Package: com.example.multifloat
Name: MultiFloat
Version: 0.18.0
Architecture: iphoneos-arm64e
Description: Multi-floating window / split-view tweak for iOS 17 (roothide / arm64e). 0.18.0 closes the remaining per-switch flash with a snapshot raise-and-mask mechanism. Archive 19 confirms the 0.17.0 mechanics all work as designed (preventive foreground keep-alive off, no-delta the dominant preventive outcome, geometry writes down from 27 to 2, tier escalation confirmed), yet the user still sees a flash on every single switch out and back. The archive pins the structural reason: every app switch drops the scene layers outright (47 real-black episodes, one per switch in both directions), the level-1 recovery write is asynchronous (400 to 800 ms), and the snapshot pad sitting at the bottom of the content area cannot mask flicker coming from the presentation view itself, which sits on top of the pad and can show black, white or a half-rendered frame during the resubmission. 0.18.0 therefore raises the snapshot to the top of the content area at the first tick a real black is detected, so the user sees the last healthy frozen frame instead of any flicker, and lowers it back to the bottom two ticks after recovery is confirmed by the render server (hosted layers back and contentState 2), so the real content covers the pad again. The pad keeps userInteractionEnabled NO, so touches pass through while it masks. The raise is skipped during the mount grace period, where the frozen frame would show stale content. New config key keepAliveSnapshotMask = true (false reverts to the pure bottom pad). Config version migrates 18 to 19.
Maintainer: QingC12 <QingC12@users.noreply.github.com>
Author: QingC12
Section: Tweaks
Depends: firmware (>= 15.0)
